+Implemented by `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` (`CallMode`: `Any`,
+`PriorityOnly`, `NormalOnly`). The action does no authorization itself yet; the
+caller is trusted and authorization is out of scope for this action. Inside one
+transaction:
+
+1. Lock the queue row (`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`) and re-read its state; reject
+   an inactive queue with `InactiveQueueException`.
+2. Choose the candidate among `waiting` tickets of the queue: priority
+   descending, then `issued_at` ascending, then `id` ascending, filtered by
+   mode. In `Any` mode the oldest normal ticket wins instead when the last three
+   calls in the queue (by `called_at`, then `id`) were all priority calls and a
+   normal ticket waits.
+3. Lock the candidate row and re-check it is still `waiting`. `Ticket::call()`
+   does not lock the queue, so a candidate can be called in between; such a
+   candidate is skipped and the next one is chosen.
+4. Set `status`, `called_at` and `called_by_user_id` on the winner and return
+   it. If nothing is eligible, throw `NoWaitingTicketsException`; the
+   transaction rolls back and nothing changes.
+
+Tests: `tests/Feature/CallNextTicketTest.php` covers ordering, modes, fairness
+and the concurrent-skip path. That race test simulates the interleaving on
+SQLite, where `lockForUpdate()` is a no-op. Real row-lock behaviour on MariaDB is
+not exercised by the automated suite.
+
